    "lawSection": "New York City",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5559\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to direct the New York state department of\nhealth to conduct a study on the high incidence of asthma in the\nborough of Manhattan in the city of New York and to prepare a remedial\nplan\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThe general purpose of the bill is to ensure that the State Department\nof Health conducts a study of the incidence of asthma in the Borough\nof Manhattan in the City of New York and provide the Governor and the\nLegislature with a remedial plan addressing high risk neighborhoods by\nexamining disparities in: income, race and ethnicity, public and\nprivate housing, proximity to major sources of air pollution, and an\nevaluation of the effectiveness of existing medical facilities.\n\n SUMMARY AND SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of this bill directs the Commissioner of the New York State\nDepartment of Health to prepare a remedial plan to address the\nincidence of asthma in the Borough of Manhattan in the City of New\nYork.\n\nSection 2 of this bill provides that a study and remedial plan shall\nbe completed within twelve months of the effective date of this act.\n\nSection 3 of this bill provides that this act shall take effect on the\none hundred twentieth day after it shall have become law. The results\nof the remedial plan shall be provided to the Governor and the\nLegislature no later than eighteen months from the beginning of such\nstudy.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nAsthma is a chronic disease of the lungs. The Center for Disease\nControl has estimated that more than more than 1.1 million adults have\nasthma in New York State. Asthma occurs at any age but is more common\nin children than adults.\n\nAccording to the New York State Department of Health, nearly one in 13\nschool-age children in the nation have asthma. The rate of prevalence\nis higher in preschool-aged children and those living in urban inner\ncities.\n\nResearch has shown that air pollution can worsen asthma symptoms. This\nis of particular concern as Manhattan has a higher than average\nincidence of air pollution.\n\nIn 2015, the New York City Department of Health and Mental Hygiene\nissued community health profiles covering all of New York City. The\nprofiles analyzed and reported on several factors including but not\nlimited to, socio-economic, health care, and neighborhood conditions.\n\nOne of the neighborhood conditions that was analyzed was air quality.\nThe reports stated, \"Although NYC air quality is improving, air\npollution, such as fine particles (PM2.5), can cause health problems,\nparticularly among the very young, seniors and those with preexisting\nhealth conditions. \"\n\nThe reports provided specific measurements of air quality in\nManhattan. The City Department of Health found that:\n\n*\"In Midtown, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful air pollutant, are\n14.3 micrograms per cubic meter, the highest in the city.\"\n\n*\"In Stuyvesant Town and Turtle Bay, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful\nair pollutant, are 12.3 micrograms per cubic meter, the second-highest\nin the city.\"\n*\"In Clinton and Chelsea, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful air\npollutant, are 11.4 micrograms per cubic meter; the district ranks\nthird-highest in the city.\"\n*\"In the Upper East Side, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful air\npollutant, are 11.1 micrograms per cubic meter, compared with 10.7 in\nManhattan and 8.6 citywide.\"\n*\"In the Financial District, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful air\npollutant, are 11.1 micrograms per cubic meter, the fourth-highest in\nthe city.\n*\"In Greenwich Village and Soho, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful air\npollutant, are 10.9 micrograms per cubic meter, compared with 10.7 in\nManhattan and 8.6 citywide.\n*\"In the Upper West Side, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful air\npollutant, are 10.3 micrograms per cubic meter, compared with 10.7 in\nManhattan and 8.6 citywide.\"\n*\"In the Lower East Side and Chinatown, levels of PM2.5, the most\nharmful air pollutant, are 9.9 micrograms per cubic meter, compared\nwith 10.7 in Manhattan and 8.6 citywide.\"\n*\"In Morningside Heights and Hamilton Heights, levels of PM2.5, the\nmost harmful air pollutant, are 9.8 micrograms per cubic meter,\ncompared with 10.7 in Manhattan and 8.6 citywide.\"\n\n*\"In East Harlem, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful air pollutant, are\n\n9.7 micrograms per cubic meter, compared with 10.7 in Manhattan and\n8.6 citywide.\"\n*\"In Central Harlem, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful air pollutant,\nare 9.6 micrograms per cubic meter, compared with 10.7 in Manhattan\nand 8.6 citywide.\"\n*\"In Washington Heights and Inwood, levels of PM2.5, the most harmful\nair pollutant, are 9.5 micrograms per cubic meter, compared with 10.7\nin Manhattan and 8.6 citywide.\"\n\n\nAll of the 2015 Manhattan based air pollutant readings were above the\ncity wide average. Due to the higher than average rates of air\npollutants found in Manhattan, it is necessary to study the rate of\nincidence of asthma in Manhattan and for the Department of Health to\nissue a remedial plan.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThe estimated cost to the State is $1 million dollars.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill shall take effect the 120th day after becoming law.\n",
